{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews","id":959184,"title":"Humanity Homebrews","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-08-28T05:43:06-07:00","id":959184,"title":"Humanity Homebrews","content":"After growing up with V:tM and never getting a chance to ST a game, I've just started my first Chronicle using V:tR 2e. I love almost all the change in Requiem and 2e, from the Clans &amp; Covenants to Masks &amp; Dirges. But I don't think Humanity is going to work for this coterie and I'd like to explore other options.<br /> <br /> Here's my issue: while I like how Humanity has moved from being a morality scale to a 'how in touch with your human life are you' scale, I don't think it is tied into other mechanics enough. Humanity should be central to every session, yet I'm struggling to make it relevant. It seems like it is up to the ST to remind/force players to take Humanity rolls and I don't think my players (who are all new to RPGs) would enjoy that. They love Aspirations, but Touchstones are a little too inflexible for their backstories.<br /> <br /> So: what alternatives to Humanity &amp; Touchstones are out there? I've heard of Atrocity dice, but my copy of Danse Macabre is still in the post. Do other STs have house rules to make Humanity more central to their games?<br /> <br /> <b>I have also been working on my own Humanity Homebrew rules and I'd love some feedback on them. You can find them here: <a href=\"http://bit.ly/vtrnewhumanity\" target=\"_blank\">http://bit.ly/vtrnewhumanity</a></b><br /> <br /> My main ideas going into this were:<ul><li>Make holding onto your Humanity about playing to your vampire's Mask</li> <li>Make gaining Experience and Willpower about playing to your vampire's Dirge</li> <li>Strongly link Aspirations to your Dirge and Mask</li> <li>Simplify Breaking Points so that they are about *monstrosity* and not morality. Using disciplines and rolling for Frenzy are breaking points but murder isn't (usually). Killing can be a human act. Moving shadows isn't.</li> <li>Humanity rolls are taken at the end of each night. As you sleep, you dwell on what you have done and wake up more (or sometimes less) a monster than the night before.</li> <li>Humanity rolls are <i>Humanity dots - BP</i>. This make it more difficult to maintain humanity the more powerful you become, but also more likely to roll successes for a starting player.</li> </ul><b>Issues I'm having</b>:<br /> - Should characters be able to redeem themselves to undo breaking points? E.g. if a character broke &amp; lost Humanity by Dominating a mortal, could they regain that humanity by freeing another mortal from their domination? Maybe this is too complex.<br /> - Are the new Breaking Points to simplified? I have removed lots of stuff about sunlight because I feel that's covered under Frenzy. And killing in the name of your Dirge should *probably* be a Breaking Point but I'm not sure where to put it...<br /> - The penalties to having a low Humanity seem weak. I want to make it clear to players that a lower Humanity means it's more likely you'll lose your Humanity next time, but the mechanics don't fit that. Losing Humanity should be a 'oh crap' moment for players.<br /> <br /> All ideas &amp; suggestions welcome!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12208-verblet","name":"verblet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T07:15:26-07:00","id":959228,"title":"","content":"You might not like my ideas here and I am sorry for that. I think it is a glass half full half empty sort of thing here. I dont think Humanity is &quot;how in touch with your human life are you' scale&quot; i think it is tapping into the monster you are becoming. Do you fight that monster or do you embrace the monster that you are? Like you said they are about monstrosity. It's not about keeping your old life the way it was, it's about not giving in to the beast inside. Over the last 3 years I have played in games as well as run VTR games. No one ever got mad when they were asked to roll a humanity. Some of my players even remind me! <br /> <br /> Humanity is tied into the Clan Banes, all other banes, conditionsTouchstones (a source of willpower ), cruac and theban as well as social rolls. When you start embracing the monster inside of you, you start to look it on the outside which gives you negs to deal with humans. Gaining banes, Conditions and all the time social negatives are for the most part, enough to keep my players from slipping into that monster mind set. GIve the new guys a shot!<ul><li>Simplify Breaking Points so that they are about *monstrosity* and not morality. Using disciplines and rolling for Frenzy are breaking points but murder isn't (usually). Killing can be a human act. Moving shadows isn't.</li> </ul>Moving in the shadows can be as well. However say killing someone with protean claws and throwing their body around is decidedly not human. I think that's what your Humanity check is for really.<br /> <br /> Humanity should be a &quot;oh crap&quot; moment or something really significant. Using disciplines is on the list, it's just higher than killing someone, as one is more monstrous than the other. <br /> <br /> Personally. I would try it out as it is before you go changing it? If you dont know what it is suppose to be like, naturally, how do you really know you dont like it?<br /> <br /> <br /> Also there have been a few people who dont like touchstones, but they dont fully understand what they are. You said you have played VTM (i never have) Have you ever made a vampire that cared about 1 person more than anyone else? One person that made you feel like you werent some terrible ... thing? Whether you knew it or not, that person would be your touchstone. Most people have touchstones built into their backstory and they dont even know it. <br /> <br /> Humanity does not need to be tested every single game with a roll. but it should sway our decisions.. Do i talk to this guy or do i pop protean and tear his face off. I choose to talk first. because I am not a monster. <br /> <br /> Nor do you need to converse with your touchstones ever single game. Do I walk into a gun fight and get shot 10 times and walk away just because I can... Or do I choose not to do that because I want the daughter I cant even see anymore to be proud of me. I want them to be proud of me because I am not a monster.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T07:45:29-07:00","id":959243,"title":"","content":"<b>Regarding your suggested house rules:</b> One issue I'm seeing is that with the dice pool being Humanity - Blood Potency, it's going to become very easy to spiral into draugurdum, especially with Humanity rolls being made every day when they sleep. The more Humanity you lose, the easier it is to lose more - which is fine, that's how it works now. But with Blood Potency as a penalty, your dice pool becomes a chance die much earlier. BP 6+ Elders wouldn't last more than a few days before going dragur, maybe weeks if they're lucky, but either way this would lead to a society of pretty much all neonates and a few ancillae, with elders all being mindless monsters. Every vampire basically has a 350 year clock, which gets shorter every time they lose humanity or gain Blood Potency by means other than age. Once it runs out, they're in the draugur death spiral.<br /> <br /> <b>Regarding the issues you're having:</b><br /> - Players can buy Humanity dots for experiences by default, and as with everything in Chronicles, Humanity purchases should be justified in the narrative. But if you wanted, you could always design Humanity-specific Beats that could be earned for adhering to your Mask or something along those lines.<br /> - Keep in mind, the list of Breaking Points in the book is only supposed to be examples. You're meant to add to, subtract from, or modify them as you see fit. Personally, I would keep the various killing Breaking Points and make doing it in defense of your Dirge incur an additional penalty to the roll (probably -1 die).<br /> - Humanity ties into a *lot* of stuff as written. Banes, including the Clan Banes, all get worse as you lose Humanity. Sunlight damage gets worse as you lose Humanity. Staying awake during the day becomes harder. The chances of creating a Revenant get worse. Torpor Duration increases. Penalties for interacting with mortals increase. Breaking Point rolls get harder, making you more likely to get a Persistent Condition from them. Losing Humanity is pretty rough as-is, maybe if I had a better idea what more you wanted it to do?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T08:21:47-07:00","id":959259,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959243#post959243\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Regarding your suggested house rules:</b> One issue I'm seeing is that with the dice pool being Humanity - Blood Potency, it's going to become very easy to spiral into draugurdum, especially with Humanity rolls being made every day when they sleep. The more Humanity you lose, the easier it is to lose more - which is fine, that's how it works now. But with Blood Potency as a penalty, your dice pool becomes a chance die much earlier. BP 6+ Elders wouldn't last more than a few days before going dragur, maybe weeks if they're lucky, but either way this would lead to a society of pretty much all neonates and a few ancillae, with elders all being mindless monsters. Every vampire basically has a 350 year clock, which gets shorter every time they lose humanity or gain Blood Potency by means other than age. Once it runs out, they're in the draugur death spiral.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I'd like to soften this a bit and would be keen on suggestions as to how (maybe switch the modfier to - BP/2?)<br /> <br /> What i was trying to achieve here was a world where Elders were very powerful but had to choose how to use that power carefully or risk losing Humanity. Also they would need to keep strong mortal links to regain their humanity once lost. It's a mechanic that encourages Elders to play their Mask and meddle in human affairs more and not get too Vampion on us. It also supports the &quot;hides from the mortal world and only strikes out when absolutely necessary&quot; archetype of Elder.<br /> <br /> The mechanics of Kindred regaining Humanity is the weakest part of this idea IMO and I'd like to work on it some more.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959243#post959243\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Regarding the issues you're having:</b><br /> - Players can buy Humanity dots for experiences by default, and as with everything in Chronicles, Humanity purchases should be justified in the narrative. But if you wanted, you could always design Humanity-specific Beats that could be earned for adhering to your Mask or something along those lines.<br /> - Keep in mind, the list of Breaking Points in the book is only supposed to be examples. You're meant to add to, subtract from, or modify them as you see fit. Personally, I would keep the various killing Breaking Points and make doing it in defense of your Dirge incur an additional penalty to the roll (probably -1 die).<br /> - Humanity ties into a *lot* of stuff as written. Banes, including the Clan Banes, all get worse as you lose Humanity. Sunlight damage gets worse as you lose Humanity. Staying awake during the day becomes harder. The chances of creating a Revenant get worse. Torpor Duration increases. Penalties for interacting with mortals increase. Breaking Point rolls get harder, making you more likely to get a Persistent Condition from them. Losing Humanity is pretty rough as-is, maybe if I had a better idea what more you wanted it to do?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, I had forgotten some of those <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> What I want to achieve is a really strong sense in my players (and me) of what causes a break in Humanity and what doesn't. What are the themes players should bear in mind when making choices? I like the move away from the morality scale of V:tM that I thought was too simplistic, but it's almost gone too far in the other direction in V:tR. I'm not confident enough, nor are my players experience enough, to make clear Humanity choices consistently, which is why I was seeking a clearer base system that we could riff on top of.<br /> <br /> For example, we have a wrestling/fighter character who has probably killed others as a mortal. They should not take killing in the name of their Mask as a breaking point, it doesn't really make sense. But to absolve that from 1 character incentivises players playing ex-gangsters and suchlike. That's why I wanted to link Breaking Points to the Dirge more. Does that make sense?<br /> <br /> I like restricting Humanity beats, I think that's what i was going for with regaining Humanity via Touchstone Aspirations.<br /> Thanks for your suggestions <span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\">\ud83d\udc4d\ud83d\udc4d</span></span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12208-verblet","name":"verblet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T08:42:29-07:00","id":959271,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959228#post959228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You might not like my ideas here and I am sorry for that. I think it is a glass half full half empty sort of thing here. I dont think Humanity is &quot;how in touch with your human life are you' scale&quot; i think it is tapping into the monster you are becoming. Do you fight that monster or do you embrace the monster that you are? Like you said they are about monstrosity. It's not about keeping your old life the way it was, it's about not giving in to the beast inside. Over the last 3 years I have played in games as well as run VTR games. No one ever got mad when they were asked to roll a humanity. Some of my players even remind me!<br /> <br /> Humanity is tied into the Clan Banes, all other banes, conditionsTouchstones (a source of willpower ), cruac and theban as well as social rolls. When you start embracing the monster inside of you, you start to look it on the outside which gives you negs to deal with humans. Gaining banes, Conditions and all the time social negatives are for the most part, enough to keep my players from slipping into that monster mind set. GIve the new guys a shot!<ul><li>Simplify Breaking Points so that they are about *monstrosity* and not morality. Using disciplines and rolling for Frenzy are breaking points but murder isn't (usually). Killing can be a human act. Moving shadows isn't.</li> </ul>Moving in the shadows can be as well. However say killing someone with protean claws and throwing their body around is decidedly not human. I think that's what your Humanity check is for really.</div> </div> </div> </div>Aye, I considered that. Killing anyone with a discipline would probably cause a Humanity roll through using that Discipline (in particular at a H7-8 level).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959228#post959228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Humanity should be a &quot;oh crap&quot; moment or something really significant. Using disciplines is on the list, it's just higher than killing someone, as one is more monstrous than the other.<br /> <br /> Personally. I would try it out as it is before you go changing it? If you dont know what it is suppose to be like, naturally, how do you really know you dont like it?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, that's a gut thing really. And it's about feeling confident in ST so I have a good basis for making decisions and communicating with players what they can and can't do without losing Humanity.<br /> <br /> I think that's the key thing here. I want my players to be confident in their choices and not be disadvantaged because they want to use Dominate and not Auspex. So making all 2-dot Discipline usage a Breaking Point is clear. Also I want them to play their Masks and work towards their mortal aspirations and give that a clear mechanical benefit. At the moment they are running wild enjoying being Vampires and I want to bring a bit of &quot;wait, stop, what would your Touchstone want&quot; to the table.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959228#post959228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also there have been a few people who dont like touchstones, but they dont fully understand what they are. You said you have played VTM (i never have) Have you ever made a vampire that cared about 1 person more than anyone else? One person that made you feel like you werent some terrible ... thing? Whether you knew it or not, that person would be your touchstone. Most people have touchstones built into their backstory and they dont even know it.<br /> <br /> Humanity does not need to be tested every single game with a roll. but it should sway our decisions.. Do i talk to this guy or do i pop protean and tear his face off. I choose to talk first. because I am not a monster.<br /> <br /> Nor do you need to converse with your touchstones ever single game. Do I walk into a gun fight and get shot 10 times and walk away just because I can... Or do I choose not to do that because I want the daughter I cant even see anymore to be proud of me. I want them to be proud of me because I am not a monster.</div> </div> </div> </div>One reason for me trying this out is that I think the existing Banes are a bit rubbish! Crossroads and Bells are just a bit too on-the-nose for me. And unless I'm mistaken the penalties to social rolls only apply to empathy stuff right? Not intimidation or manipulation?<br /> <br /> I think I might have gone a bit too far in changing stuff for a first draft, but I'm especially keen on tying Humanity to Aspirations, Touchstones, and Masks. I love the idea of Touchstones but my current characters are all distant from theirs and need some motivation to consider them in their choices.<br /> <br /> Any reason why experienced players think that's a non-starter?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12208-verblet","name":"verblet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T09:36:56-07:00","id":959304,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959259#post959259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, I'd like to soften this a bit and would be keen on suggestions as to how (maybe switch the modfier to - BP/2?)</div> </div> </div> </div>BP/2 might be a little too far in the opposite direction... What about using the default dice pools, but tying them to Blood Potency instead of Humanity? Although then the dice pools wouldn't shrink as your Humaniry dropped... Hmm... Maybe you could go with the default rolls and stack an additional bonus/penalty based on high/medium/low Blood Potency?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959259#post959259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What i was trying to achieve here was a world where Elders were very powerful but had to choose how to use that power carefully or risk losing Humanity. Also they would need to keep strong mortal links to regain their humanity once lost. It's a mechanic that encourages Elders to play their Mask and meddle in human affairs more and not get too Vampion on us. It also supports the &amp;quot;hides from the mortal world and only strikes out when absolutely necessary&amp;quot; archetype of Elder.</div> </div> </div> </div>I kinda feel like the rules as-written already do most of what you're looking for... Having to maintain strong mortal links to maintain Humanity is the exactly the function of Touchstones. The example Breaking Points for X amount of time without human contact is also supposed to keep them from totally retreating from human society. As for forcing them to be careful about how they use their power... The high end of the scale has some example Breaking Points related to that, like &quot;spending more than one Vitae in a night&quot;, &quot;urging another's behavior with a Discipline&quot;, and &quot;depriving another of consent with a Discipline.&quot; You could always make scaled-up versions of these Breaking Points to put further down the scale. Maybe spending more than 10 Vitae in a night is a Humanity 5 Breaking Point, spending more than 20 is a Humanity 1 Breaking Point or something. Maybe depriving another of Consent with a Discipline is always a Breaking Point, with a base dice pool of 4, modified by the severity of the act you forced them to do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959259#post959259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I want to achieve is a really strong sense in my players (and me) of what causes a break in Humanity and what doesn't. What are the themes players should bear in mind when making choices? I like the move away from the morality scale of V:tM that I thought was too simplistic, but it's almost gone too far in the other direction in V:tR. I'm not confident enough, nor are my players experience enough, to make clear Humanity choices consistently, which is why I was seeking a clearer base system that we could riff on top of.<br /> <br /> For example, we have a wrestling/fighter character who has probably killed others as a mortal. They should not take killing in the name of their Mask as a breaking point, it doesn't really make sense. But to absolve that from 1 character incentivises players playing ex-gangsters and suchlike. That's why I wanted to link Breaking Points to the Dirge more. Does that make sense?</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, I gotcha. The way I read it, Humanity represents how human a character's worldview is. Breaking Points happen when the character is forced to face the fact that they are definitely not human. Humanity loss happens when they rationalize the act that forced them to face their own monstrosity, incorporating it into their worldview as something that's normal for them. So, going with your killing example, it's normal for humans to feel guilt or remorse when they kill other humans. Or, if nothing else, they might at least feel justified in their actions, but they at least feel <i>something</i>, barring people with severe empathy disorders. When a vampire kills a human, though, they don't feel anything. That cognitive dissonance of knowing &quot;I just killed someone. I should feel bad about this. Why don't I feel anything?&quot; forced them to face the fact that they've become a monster, so they roll a Breaking Point. If they succeed, they understand they've done something that should make them feel guilty, and they can at least feel guilty about not feeling guilty, even if the act itself didn't elicit any emotions. If they fail, they just accept that not feeling anything about it is ok - after all, they never felt guilty about eating hamburgers as a human, why should this be any different? Their worldview twists to preserve their comfort, and in doing so they become a little bit more distanced from a human way of thinking, a little bit more monstrous.<br /> <br /> This is also why acts stop being Breaking Points as you lose humanity - in your mind, those things are perfectly normal, so they don't challenge your worldview anymore.<br /> <br /> I would recommend going through the list of example Breaking Points under this lense - Humanity as &quot;how human your worldview is&quot;, and Breaking Points as &quot;things that shake your worldview&quot;, success indicating that you maintain a human perspective on yourself and failure indicating that your way of thinking changes in response to whatever challenged it and becomes a little bit less human. Decide which ones you want to keep and which ones you want to throw out. Think about what other acts you might want to add to the list, and formulate a master list you can use in your game. I would also recommend getting your players involved in this process, discussing what acts they think should or shouldn't count as Breaking Points and why.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T10:06:23-07:00","id":959317,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959271#post959271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One reason for me trying this out is that I think the existing Banes are a bit rubbish! Crossroads and Bells are just a bit too on-the-nose for me.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sure what you mean by on the nose?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959271#post959271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And unless I'm mistaken the penalties to social rolls only apply to empathy stuff right? Not intimidation or manipulation?</div> </div> </div> </div>No, it applies to any rolls to interact with mortals. Doesn't specify anything about particular skills or attributes being exceptions. As your worldview becomes less and less human, it becomes harder and harder not only to understand humans, but to manipulate or coerce them, as you begin to forget what matters to them and how to leverage them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959271#post959271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I might have gone a bit too far in changing stuff for a first draft, but I'm especially keen on tying Humanity to Aspirations, Touchstones, and Masks. I love the idea of Touchstones but my current characters are all distant from theirs and need some motivation to consider them in their choices.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, Humanity and Touchstones are already quite intimately linked. As for Aspirations and <br /> Masks, I would recommend taking a leaf from Mage's book. Make it so that you can only raise Humanity after spending at least a chapter with &quot;get more in touch with my human side&quot; as a long-term Aspiration. Make it require Humanity Experiences to increase, and make setbacks in persuit of one's Mask one of the ways to gain Humanity Beats.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959271#post959271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Any reason why experienced players think that's a non-starter?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not at all, I think you've got some good ideas, they just need a little refining. I do think practical experience with running the system as written would be a big help in that department, but fortunately there are a lot of people here with such experience that can help advise you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T11:08:53-07:00","id":959339,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959317#post959317\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm sure what you mean by on the nose?</div> </div> </div> </div>Sorry I mean too precise. Like, stereotypically vampire-y to the point of silliness. I would prefer if Banes were linked to the Breaking Points. So failing a BP on sunlight and losing Humanity gave you a Light Sensitivity bane, or murdering a friend gave you flashbacks or apparitions of your victim. That kind of thing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959317#post959317\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, it applies to any rolls to interact with mortals. Doesn't specify anything about particular skills or attributes being exceptions. As your worldview becomes less and less human, it becomes harder and harder not only to understand humans, but to manipulate or coerce them, as you begin to forget what matters to them and how to leverage them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hmm, okay. That does make it more undesirable, but that's not what I took from the passage in 2e:<br /> &quot;Also, there are minor Social modifiers listed. These modifiers apply to rolls relating to humans. <b>Manipulating humans for food with Subterfuge or frightening them with Intimidation<br /> are not affected</b>; but trying to understand a human\u2019s emotional cues with the Empathy Skill would be affected&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959317#post959317\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, Humanity and Touchstones are already quite intimately linked. As for Aspirations and<br /> Masks, I would recommend taking a leaf from Mage's book. Make it so that you can only raise Humanity after spending at least a chapter with &quot;get more in touch with my human side&quot; as a long-term Aspiration. Make it require Humanity Experiences to increase, and make setbacks in persuit of one's Mask one of the ways to gain Humanity Beats.<br /> Not at all, I think you've got some good ideas, they just need a little refining. I do think practical experience with running the system as written would be a big help in that department, but fortunately there are a lot of people here with such experience that can help advise you.</div> </div> </div> </div>Aye, I think at the start I will go with the Humanity Beats idea + adding all Discipline use to the Breaking Point scale somewhere (I don't want to give Auspex &amp; Protean users a benefit over Majesty &amp; Domination which seem to force Humanity rolls from what I can see).<br /> <br /> I'll see how it goes \ud83d\udc4d. thanks for the advice so far","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12208-verblet","name":"verblet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T11:18:45-07:00","id":959343,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959339#post959339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sorry I mean too precise. Like, stereotypically vampire-y to the point of silliness. I would prefer if Banes were linked to the Breaking Points. So failing a BP on sunlight and losing Humanity gave you a Light Sensitivity bane, or murdering a friend gave you flashbacks or apparitions of your victim. That kind of thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ok, I understand. Yeah, Banes are supposed to be folkloric vampire weaknesses, the idea being that by internalizing an inhuman act to the point that it will never shake your worldview again permanently makes you more monstrous. It's also probably where those folklore weaknesses came from - people telling stories of surviving an encounter with a vampire by scattering rice that they then had to stop and count, and incorrectly interpreting them as inherent qualities all vampires share. But, if you don't care for them, that's fair.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959339#post959339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hmm, okay. That does make it more undesirable, but that's not what I took from the passage in 2e:<br /> &amp;quot;Also, there are minor Social modifiers listed. These modifiers apply to rolls relating to humans. <b>Manipulating humans for food with Subterfuge or frightening them with Intimidation<br /> are not affected</b>; but trying to understand a human\u2019s emotional cues with the Empathy Skill would be affected&amp;quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh... You're right, I must have misremembered. I guess it does only apply to rolls to understand humans. Nonetheless, I would say it applies to any rolls to empathize with them, not only rolls inclvolving the Empathy Skill.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959339#post959339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Aye, I think at the start I will go with the Humanity Beats idea + adding all Discipline use to the Breaking Point scale somewhere (I don't want to give Auspex &amp; Protean users a benefit over Majesty &amp; Domination which seem to force Humanity rolls from what I can see).<br /> <br /> I'll see how it goes \ud83d\udc4d. thanks for the advice so far</div> </div> </div> </div>Sounds good! Best of luck with it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T11:39:12-07:00","id":959353,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959304#post959304\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">BP/2 might be a little too far in the opposite direction... What about using the default dice pools, but tying them to Blood Potency instead of Humanity? Although then the dice pools wouldn't shrink as your Humaniry dropped... Hmm... Maybe you could go with the default rolls and stack an additional bonus/penalty based on high/medium/low Blood Potency?</div> </div> </div> </div>Right, I've just realised I've been reading the rules incorrectly. I assumed that the dice pool related to the Humanity level of the character and not the Breaking Point level.<br /> &quot;Roll the number of dice associated with the breaking point\u2019s level&quot; makes sense now! <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959304#post959304\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah, I gotcha. The way I read it, Humanity represents how human a character's worldview is. Breaking Points happen when the character is forced to face the fact that they are definitely not human. Humanity loss happens when they rationalize the act that forced them to face their own monstrosity, incorporating it into their worldview as something that's normal for them. So, going with your killing example, it's normal for humans to feel guilt or remorse when they kill other humans. Or, if nothing else, they might at least feel justified in their actions, but they at least feel <i>something</i>, barring people with severe empathy disorders. When a vampire kills a human, though, they don't feel anything. That cognitive dissonance of knowing &quot;I just killed someone. I should feel bad about this. Why don't I feel anything?&quot; forced them to face the fact that they've become a monster, so they roll a Breaking Point. If they succeed, they understand they've done something that should make them feel guilty, and they can at least feel guilty about not feeling guilty, even if the act itself didn't elicit any emotions. If they fail, they just accept that not feeling anything about it is ok - after all, they never felt guilty about eating hamburgers as a human, why should this be any different? Their worldview twists to preserve their comfort, and in doing so they become a little bit more distanced from a human way of thinking, a little bit more monstrous.<br /> <br /> This is also why acts stop being Breaking Points as you lose humanity - in your mind, those things are perfectly normal, so they don't challenge your worldview anymore.<br /> <br /> I would recommend going through the list of example Breaking Points under this lense - Humanity as &quot;how human your worldview is&quot;, and Breaking Points as &quot;things that shake your worldview&quot;, success indicating that you maintain a human perspective on yourself and failure indicating that your way of thinking changes in response to whatever challenged it and becomes a little bit less human. Decide which ones you want to keep and which ones you want to throw out. Think about what other acts you might want to add to the list, and formulate a master list you can use in your game. I would also recommend getting your players involved in this process, discussing what acts they think should or shouldn't count as Breaking Points and why.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ah okay, this is a lot clearer now. I've been viewing Humanity as a scale of transgression and monstrosity - indications that you're no longer human and how comfortable you are with that.<br /> <br /> I would still like a way to personalise this for each character so that their Mask is a reflection of this worldview rather than 'a lie I tell mortals' which is how it is presented in 2e, if that makes sense?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12208-verblet","name":"verblet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T12:29:09-07:00","id":959370,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959353#post959353\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Right, I've just realised I've been reading the rules incorrectly. I assumed that the dice pool related to the Humanity level of the character and not the Breaking Point level.<br /> &quot;Roll the number of dice associated with the breaking point\u2019s level&quot; makes sense now!</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, that's an easy mistake to make. Especially since it works the opposite way for a lot of other Integrity-equivalent stats.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959353#post959353\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Ah okay, this is a lot clearer now. I've been viewing Humanity as a scale of transgression and monstrosity - indications that you're no longer human and how comfortable you are with that.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's sorta that too. That's CofD for you, the line between the literal and the figurative is very blurry. Breaking Points are definitely indications that you're not human. &quot;Holy crap, I just got hit by a bus and I don't have a scratch on me&quot;, &quot;holy crap, I just looked a guy I was in an argument with in the eye and told him to to blow me and he's actually about to do it&quot;, &quot;holy crap I just drank someone's blood until they collapsed on the ground and I don't feel a shred of remorse&quot;, etc. None of these things are human behaviors, and they force you to confront the fact that you're definitely not human any more (trigger a Breaking Point). And in a sense it's how comfortable you are with that - if you convince yourself that of course the bus wouldn't harm you much, you <i>are</i> immortal after all; of course the guy is going to blow you when you say to, you're a Lord and Lords are meant to be obeyed without question; of course you don't feel bad about killing this person, you're at the top of the food chain and they're your food, lions don't feel bad about eating gazelles. That's when your Humanity drops, because your worldview is becoming less human. And, because CofD, a monstrous worldview makes you more of a literal monster. Your vampiric weaknesses get harsher, you find it harder to empathize with humans, and if you truly, deeply internalize the idea that it's normal for you to do these things because you're a vampire and that's what vampires do, you might even develop an inability to enter houses without an invitation. But, if you try to cling to normalcy, if you let yourself be horrified by how far removed from human you really are, you maintain your Humanity a little longer.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959353#post959353\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would still like a way to personalise this for each character so that their Mask is a reflection of this worldview rather than 'a lie I tell mortals' which is how it is presented in 2e, if that makes sense?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, it's not just a lie they tell mortals. It's a lie they tell each other. It's a lie they tell <i>themselves</i>. The Masquerade isn't just a defense mechanism to keep the mortal flock from finding out about vampires. It's an elaborate facade that vampires participate in so they can preserve their own delusions of humanity. We're nothing like those mindless, slavering Draugur, because we have a society and rules. We're nothing like those cruel, pitiless Strix because we have bodies, and ethics. We're nothing like those demon-worshiping Belial's Brood, because we respect the Traditions and fear the Gods. As long as we follow the rules and act like humans, we can have normal lives. Just because we're not human doesn't mean we can't be humane. Just because we're immortal doesn't mean we have to be immoral. Just because we're monsters doesn't mean we can't be people. Your Mask is the face you put on that makes those lies easier to believe. Conveniently, it also makes it easier to hide among your human prey.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 08-28-2016, 03:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-28T12:29:44-07:00","id":959371,"title":"","content":"Charlaquinn had some really great advice for you. The key point I took away from reading all that is. Humanity already does what you want it to do. As written it already does every thing you want it to do.<br /> <br /> The clan banes are suppose to be sort of stereotypical as are the clans. There are more personalized banes for personalized flavor, but a good little vampire might never even encounter these. <br /> Unless your players are functioning at 8-10 Humanity range, Majesty, Dominate and well any other discipline doesnt force a humanity check. Now if you use it to have Joe kill Jane for you, then it would, but it would be down at Murdering someone. <br /> <br /> I take it as you accepting the beast, but like Charlaquinn says &quot;how your world view is&quot; I believe those are the same thing, just different words. If you accept that you are a monster and are going to do monstrous things, your worldview is going to be a bit jaded and going to take a lot more to shake it up. <br /> <br /> <br /> As far as the mask, my group doesnt play it &quot;A lie I tell mortals&quot; but more like... The face you show in public vs who you are when no one is looking. like... You're different when you're with your friends vs coworkers or classmates. We play it so much more fluid than a literal mask. If it helps have your players think of it more as a personality trait instead of something that feels fake and forced. You know if you're a junkie and everyone knows you're a junkie, you dont have to run around telling everyone you are just to get that validation. You're friends know you are just going to buy that lottery ticket at every gas station you pass. It's just who you are, they know you cant help it. You just have to. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-29T16:46:49-07:00","id":960099,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=959370#post959370\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;holy crap I just drank someone's blood until they collapsed on the ground and I don't feel a shred of remorse&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Just a quick question. Is this an example of a thought process or the expected thought process?<br /> <br /> I ask because I sometimes discover small details in these forums, and while I can't remember a ruling that vampires lose that emotion reaction to murder (only feeling bad in a reflective way), I wouldn't be the least bit surprised at this point that I missed it.<br /> <br /> (Currently, I thought the play was more like &quot;Oh shit, oh shit, he's dead! What do I do? ... Nothing... It's fine. He was a bad guy, and the pastor said we were 'damnation to the kine'... yeah, he deserved to die. I'm just playing my part. I just need to stash the body. It's all okay...&quot;)<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2003-tabanese","name":"Tabanese","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2003_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"282"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-29T16:59:12-07:00","id":960103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tabanese</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=960099#post960099\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Just a quick question. Is this an example of a thought process or the expected thought process?<br /> <br /> I ask because I sometimes discover small details in these forums, and while I can't remember a ruling that vampires lose that emotion reaction to murder (only feeling bad in a reflective way), I wouldn't be the least bit surprised at this point that I missed it.<br /> <br /> (Currently, I thought the play was more like &amp;quot;Oh shit, oh shit, he's dead! What do I do? ... Nothing... It's fine. He was a bad guy, and the pastor said we were 'damnation to the kine'... yeah, he deserved to die. I'm just playing my part. I just need to stash the body. It's all okay...&amp;quot<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>As far as I know, loss of emotional reaction to murder isn't explicitly called out in the text, it's just my personal interpretation. And, truthfully, I dramatized my own interpretation for the sake of the example. In fact, I wouldn't tell a player how their character reacts (or doesn't react, as the case may be) emotionally to something, that's not my job as ST. What I would do is try to express through narration that their Beast is satisfied or elated by it - especially if the death was caused by feeding - and that unless the character was already a complete monster before being embraced, this reaction causes the sort of cognitive dissonance that I see as triggering a Breaking Point against Humanity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-29T18:23:56-07:00","id":960152,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=960103#post960103\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> As far as I know, loss of emotional reaction to murder isn't explicitly called out in the text, it's just my personal interpretation. And, truthfully, I dramatized my own interpretation for the sake of the example. In fact, I wouldn't tell a player how their character reacts (or doesn't react, as the case may be) emotionally to something, that's not my job as ST. What I would do is try to express through narration that their Beast is satisfied or elated by it - especially if the death was caused by feeding - and that unless the character was already a complete monster before being embraced, this reaction causes the sort of cognitive dissonance that I see as triggering a Breaking Point against Humanity.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I agree with Charlaquin here. Always has great info ( even if we dont always agree on it ~.^ ) <br /> <br /> Telling a player how to feel is rarely ever the right way to go. ( unless they have just been blood bonded, .. different ball game there ) How they should and should not feel about a breaking point is really up to them and where they are on the humanity scale. if they are humanity 10 and just killed someone, Yes they should feel like a monster, but if they are humanity 3 and killed someone in self defense, they wouldnt react the same way as someone else. We all need to interpret our character's emotions for ourselves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-30T08:33:05-07:00","id":960455,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the advice everyone, it is all very useful.<br /> <br /> The part about 'satisfying the Beast' in particular is useful to distinguish immoral acts that trigger Breaking Points from those that do not. Even someone who committed murder as a mortal will feel the pull of the Beast as inhuman and overwhelming.<br /> <br /> My aim here is to give my players clear guidance and a rules basis for their storytelling so no-one feels disadvantaged for their character choices or unsure about the consequences of their actions.<br /> <br /> Rather than rework the Humanity rules right now I think I'll stick with them as they are &amp; offer some player guidance:<br /> <br /> - Acting to your Mask (or to aid a Touchstone) should rarely cause a breaking point.<br /> - Violence in the name of the Beast or your Drige is likely to cause a breaking point.<br /> - We will come up with Banes on the fly based on your character and situation.<br /> - It's at ST discretion on what is a breaking point, but in the player's hands how your character responds to it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12208-verblet","name":"verblet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-30T08:35:01-07:00","id":960458,"title":"","content":"I'm also thinking about starting a Play-By-Play thread for our Chronicle, mostly as a learning experience us and other newbies. Is anyone doing something similar? Would it be useful?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12208-verblet","name":"verblet","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-30T10:12:08-07:00","id":960520,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=960458#post960458\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm also thinking about starting a Play-By-Play thread for our Chronicle, mostly as a learning experience us and other newbies. Is anyone doing something similar? Would it be useful?</div> </div> </div> </div>I would be interested to see how it turns out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3268-krzyzstof","name":"Krzyzstof","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3268_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-30T14:11:20-07:00","id":960661,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>verblet</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/959184-humanity-homebrews?p=960455#post960455\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks for the advice everyone, it is all very useful.<br /> <br /> The part about 'satisfying the Beast' in particular is useful to distinguish immoral acts that trigger Breaking Points from those that do not. Even someone who committed murder as a mortal will feel the pull of the Beast as inhuman and overwhelming.<br /> <br /> My aim here is to give my players clear guidance and a rules basis for their storytelling so no-one feels disadvantaged for their character choices or unsure about the consequences of their actions.<br /> <br /> Rather than rework the Humanity rules right now I think I'll stick with them as they are &amp; offer some player guidance:<br /> <br /> - Acting to your Mask (or to aid a Touchstone) should rarely cause a breaking point.<br /> - Violence in the name of the Beast or your Drige is likely to cause a breaking point.<br /> - We will come up with Banes on the fly based on your character and situation.<br /> - It's at ST discretion on what is a breaking point, but in the player's hands how your character responds to it.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I think this is a great start. However if your mask is a psycho, you are still going to hit breaking points. Or if your touchstone is a bad influence and asks you to do terrible things, that could still be breaking points too. Violence for the sake of violence ( beast or dirge ) is yes often a breaking point. But remember if that violence is just going all out protean, claws and all and trashing your bedroom because you are having a fit and want to break stuff... probably shouldnt be.<br /> <br /> As far as banes go, what Helps my group and the story flow better. If they hit a breaking point and want to take one ( because they are a choice ... not clan bane, but normal banes ) I let them have until next session to work something out. You dont have to take it right then and there, stopping the game to do so. In the narrative, it's like really letting what happened sink in. Really scar them to the core.<br /> <br /> yes yes yes to the last one. The only thing I might add is, I make my players auto fail if they simply dont feel remorse or regret or that they were wrong for doing what they did. ( or for what the beast made them do )<br /> <br /> <br /> Example:<br /> One of my players, who is playing someone like Dexter. He joined the Ordo and Started experimenting on humans. He thinks this shouldn't be a problem. It is a neccessary thing that needs to be done for the sake of learning. it didn't fitting for him to have to roll a breaking point every time he went to work, because clearly he feels there is nothing wrong with it. So he autodropped from 7 to 6 and chose to take a bane.<br /> <br /> other characters, simply wouldnt be able to stomach that and therefore it should be a breaking point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}